INT. ALL-NIGHT PHARMACY - COUNTER - 2 A.M.

Flickering fluorescents. Rows of cough syrup. A sleepy rotating display of sunglasses. Rain streaks the front window. A bell above the door.

ELIAS VOLKOV, 50s, pharmacist, white coat, half-moon glasses, finishes counting pills into a vial. GRETA HOLT, 30s, a damp coat, a leather satchel, walks in, shakes off her umbrella, and sets a prescription slip on the counter.

ELIAS
Evening.

GRETA
Morning, technically.

ELIAS
Technically.

He reads the slip. Pauses. Looks at her over the glasses.

ELIAS (CONT'D)
This is for ten milligrams.

GRETA
Yes.

ELIAS
Of a rather strong sedative.

GRETA
My doctor says I sleep poorly.

ELIAS
Who is your doctor?

GRETA
Dr. Peary.

ELIAS
Dr. Peary retired in March.

GRETA
Did he?

ELIAS
He sent me a card.

GRETA
Lovely. Is the medication ready?

ELIAS
Give me a moment.

He disappears into the back. The bell above the door tinkles gently in the draft. Greta takes out a phone and sets it on the counter, screen down. She flips it over. Flips it back. Behind the shelves, drawers open and close.

ELIAS (O.S.)
(calling)
How long have you had trouble sleeping?

GRETA
Years.

ELIAS (O.S.)
Years.

GRETA
Yes.

Elias reappears with a paper bag. He sets it on the counter between them. Neither touches it.

ELIAS
Ms. Holt. May I be frank?

GRETA
Please.

ELIAS
You do not look like a woman who sleeps poorly.

GRETA
What do I look like?

ELIAS
A woman who has not slept at all.

Greta says nothing. A car swishes by outside.

ELIAS (CONT'D)
The man who was in here last night. The one with the cough.

GRETA
I don't know him.

ELIAS
He said the same of you.

Greta's hand moves to the satchel. Elias's hand moves under the counter. Both freeze. Neither breathes. Rain drums on the glass.

GRETA
Mr. Volkov.

ELIAS
Ms. Holt.

GRETA
I'd like my medication.

ELIAS
And I'd like to see what's in the satchel.

She unlatches it slowly. Elias slides his hand a little further. She tips the satchel toward him. Inside: bundles of cash. He gazes at them. At her.

ELIAS (CONT'D)
That is a great deal of money for a sedative.

GRETA
It's for the man with the cough.

ELIAS
He's dead, Ms. Holt.

GRETA
I know.

She pulls the satchel shut. Elias pulls a small revolver from under the counter. Sets it down on the pills.

ELIAS
Then who is the money for?

GRETA
You.
